---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PngToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PngToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="png-to-pdf">
  <PngToPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert PNG images to a PDF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Add your PNG files: drop them on the box above or click to browse. Your device processes them.</li>
      <li>Arrange the pages by dragging the cards, or with the arrow buttons on each card for keyboard users.</li>
      <li>Pick <strong>Auto</strong> to give every page the exact proportions of its image, or a fixed size such as A4 for a uniform document.</li>
      <li>Adjust orientation, margin and fit if you chose a fixed page size.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Create PDF</strong> and download the result.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What happens to transparency</h2>
    <p>
      PNG supports an alpha channel; PDF pages do not have a background layer at
      all. When a transparent PNG is placed on a page, the transparent pixels
      show what is behind them. Behind them is nothing, which all PDF viewers and
      printers show as white paper.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice that means a logo with a transparent background will look correct
      on a normal white page and will <em>not</em> stay transparent if you later
      place the PDF over a coloured background. If you need a specific backdrop,
      flatten the PNG onto that colour before converting. The image data itself is
      kept in both cases: the alpha channel is embedded, but there is nothing behind
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>Screenshots: a practical recipe</h2>
    <p>
      Screenshots are the most common PNG input, and they are usually all different
      sizes. Two setups work well:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Goal</th>
            <th>Page size</th>
            <th>Fit</th>
            <th>Margin</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Compact, no borders, one screenshot per page</td>
            <td>Auto</td>
            <td>None</td>
            <td>0 mm</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Printable report with consistent pages</td>
            <td>A4 or Letter</td>
            <td>Fit</td>
            <td>12–15 mm</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Wide UI captures that should not shrink</td>
            <td>A4 with Landscape</td>
            <td>Fit</td>
            <td>8 mm</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Is the PNG re-compressed?</h2>
    <p>
      No. PNG is one of the two image formats a PDF can carry natively, so the
      original bytes are embedded unchanged. The pages are pixel-identical to your
      files, and the finished PDF is approximately the sum of the input sizes plus
      a couple of kilobytes of document structure. Nothing is resampled, softened
      or watermarked.
    </p>
    <p>
      That also means a PDF built from PNGs can be large. A dozen full-resolution
      desktop screenshots easily reach 20 MB. If size matters more than pixel
      fidelity, convert the screenshots to JPG first and use
      <a href="/jpg-to-pdf">JPG to PDF</a>, or run the finished document through a
      <a href="/compress-pdf">PDF compressor</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Everything stays local</h2>
    <p>
      Decoding and PDF assembly both happen in this browser tab using pdf-lib. No
      image is uploaded, so screenshots containing internal dashboards, customer
      data or credentials stay on your machine. The tool also works with the
      network disconnected.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
